{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1787774744,"nonce":"oRbHx0oWAWtWRT9AF+vn2KavyKg=","title":"ADR-0061: workspace data path — workspace service + node driver, lazy materialisation","message":"\u003e Imported from pull request #93 (MERGED)\n\u003e Branch: `docs/adr-0061-workspace-data-path` → `main`\n\u003e Created: 2026-07-26T17:59:33Z · Merged: 2026-08-25T05:27:44Z\n\nDesign-only PR. Records the decision; **no implementation**. Implementation lands on this same branch/PR in follow-up commits.\n\n\nADR-0004 promised a control-plane / data-plane split. The implementation doesn't honour it: `drive_workspace` moves the whole workspace **through the Kubernetes API server** via `kubectl exec` tar — `tar -xf` in (`lib.rs:450`), `tar -cf` out (`lib.rs:473`) — and hashes it in `scarab-server`. ADR-0029's dedup applies only *after* that, between the server and object storage.\n\nThat path is unsound by inspection, independent of any benchmark: a single unresumable stream, through the cluster's most contended component, funnelled via one process that must hold and hash every byte, moving the entire workspace both directions regardless of what changed.\n\nUnder the target substrate — **Karpenter + spot** — every Step boundary is a network boundary and co-location cannot be assumed. That rules out the topologies other engines use to make this disappear (GHA's job, Woodpecker's pipeline volume, Tekton's affinity assistant).\n\n\n1. **Workspace service** — long-lived, per failure domain, warm CAS on a persistent volume. The PVC attaches to *it*, never to a Step Pod. Generic object storage behind it as the cold archive under a TTL. **Standard path in every deployment mode** — no accelerator/fallback split.\n2. **Scarab node driver** — mounts a Workspace Snapshot read-only with a pod-local writable layer. **Lazy**: a Step transfers what it reads, not what it inherits.\n3. **Control plane leaves the data path** — the server exchanges root hashes. The `exec` tar tunnel is deleted; `busybox` doorstops become Scarab-owned images.\n4. **An Attempt is not `Succeeded` until its Workspace Snapshot is durable.**\n\n\n\u003e Minimise the substrate idiosyncrasies an author must know. Where the substrate is expensive, **the system pays, not the author.**\n\nThis is why the implicit inherit-everything default is permanent, why `outputs:` stays a precision tool rather than a performance tax, and why \"don't put caches in the workspace\" is retired as advice — all unenforceable substrate knowledge leaking into other people's YAML.\n\n\n`CONTEXT.md` conflated two things under **Workspace**:\n\n- **Workspace** — the *mutable* filesystem a Step executes in; pod-local, dies with the Pod.\n- **Workspace Snapshot** — the *immutable* content-addressed tree on the DAG edge, owned by an Attempt as evidence.\n\n\n| file | change |\n|---|---|\n| `docs/adr/0061-workspace-data-path.md` | new |\n| `docs/adr/0004-execution-topology.md` | data path superseded; topology stands; \"second and last stateful dependency\" retracted |\n| `docs/adr/0007-data-passing-model.md` | `outputs:` is precision, never a performance tax |\n| `CONTEXT.md` | §4.2 five → six concepts; Workspace / Workspace Snapshot split |\n| `docs/adr/README.md` | index |\n\n\n- **Not built.**\n- **No numbers taken.** The ADR rests on the structural argument, *not* on an unmeasured claim that the tunnel dominates today. Measurement is scoped to sizing the win and sequencing slices.\n- **A third stateful component**, accepted deliberately — ADR-0004's \"second (and last)\" is retracted in place.\n- **A privileged node driver becomes an install prerequisite**, including on kind/colima. This is a *larger* ask than Woodpecker's (a default StorageClass) and the ADR says so.\n- Rejected alternatives are recorded at length (PVC per step, RWO per run, RWX, volume cloning, git-as-store, coarsening the unit, node-cache-only, fat-helper-without-driver) so they don't get re-proposed.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\n---\n\n\n**@thulasi-ram** — 2026-07-26T18:01:50Z\n\n\nTracked in git-bug, not GitHub issues. Pushed to `refs/bugs`. `git bug bug ls` is broken in this repo — read them with `git bug bug show --field title \u003cid\u003e` / `git bug bug show \u003cid\u003e`.\n\n| id | slice | depends on |\n|---|---|---|\n| `d68a336` | s0 — measure the current workspace data path | — |\n| `6b70425` | s1 — workspace service (warm CAS, standard path) | — |\n| `0057d4a` | s2 — node driver: lazy read-only mount + writable layer | s1 |\n| `212bb13` | s3 — delete the exec tar tunnel; server exchanges root hashes only | s1, s2 |\n| `6b39fa2` | s4 — an Attempt is not Succeeded until its snapshot is durable | s3 |\n| `cc154d7` | s5 — two-tier retention: space-bounded warm, time-bounded cold | — |\n| `66fc0e3` | s6 — *follow-up* overlay-diff drain (hash only the writable layer) | s2, s3 |\n| `3800e86` | s7 — verify mtime/permission fidelity through the CAS | do before s3 |\n| `4f15a66` | s8 — propagate Workspace vs Workspace Snapshot naming | alongside s3 |\n\nImplementation lands on this same branch/PR.\n\nTwo sequencing notes worth honouring: **s7 before s3** (the `tar` legs preserve mtimes and permissions — check whether the CAS does *before* deleting them), and **s0 early** (it doesn't gate the decision, but it sizes the win and settles whether a second-level node cache earns its place).\n\n**@thulasi-ram** — 2026-07-26T19:01:12Z\n\n\nTwo things changed the plan since the table above was written: **s0's numbers\nlanded** (`bd30a71`) and said the `exec` tar tunnel is only **4–15%** of a Step\nboundary while the CAS legs are **81–88%**, and a red-team scoping pass on s1/s2\nconcluded the driver is not a one-session slice. So the sequence is no longer\n\"delete the tunnel, then get clever\".\n\n**Revised sequencing:**\n\n```\ns7 (done) → s0 (done, numbers committed) → s1 → s3-feed → [deferred: s2b → s3-drain → s6]\n```\n\n| slice | what | state |\n|---|---|---|\n| **s7** | CAS metadata fidelity — `mode` + `mtime_ms` on `TreeEntry`, symlink as blob + `MODE_SYMLINK` | **done** — `dd67e12`, standing proof in `crates/scarab-storage-s3/tests/fidelity.rs` |\n| **s0** | time and size the Step-boundary data path | **done** — `707b891`, numbers **committed to the ADR's Open section** in `bd30a71` |\n| **s1** | workspace service (`Role::Workspace` on `scarab-server`) + `TieredCas` + `ContentSource` + `scarab-workspace-client` + token + Helm StatefulSet/PVC | next |\n| **s3-feed** | replace the **feed** tunnel with a Scarab-owned fetcher image (`docker/wsfetch/`) pulling from the service | with/after s1 |\n| **s2b** | CSI ephemeral inline volume + FUSE lazy lower layer — the actual node driver | **deferred**, ticket `0628369` |\n| **s3-drain** | replace the **drain** tunnel **and the artifact-harvest tunnel** | **deferred**, ticket `7f05f39` |\n| **s6** | overlay-diff drain — hash only the writable layer | **deferred**, ticket `66fc0e3` (comment added: now depends on s2b, and on s2b's overlay mount options) |\n| **s4** | an Attempt is not `Succeeded` until its snapshot is durable | ticket `6b39fa2` — **already written**, not yet built |\n| **s5** | warm-tier retention | **split, see below** |\n| **s8** | propagate Workspace vs Workspace Snapshot naming | ticket `4f15a66` — **already written**, not yet built |\n\n\nThat collision was the highest-probability way for two implementation agents to\nclobber each other in `crates/scarab-executor-k8s/src/lib.rs`. Settled\nvocabulary, used above and in every ticket:\n\n- **s3-feed** — replace the *feed* tunnel with the Scarab-owned fetcher. This\n  **is** the eager stepping stone; it is not a separate \"s2a\".\n- **s3-drain** — replace the *drain* tunnel **and** the third `exec` tar in\n  `harvest_artifacts` (`lib.rs:673`), which ADR-0061 never mentioned.\n- **s2b** — the CSI/FUSE node driver.\n\nOne agent owns `crates/scarab-executor-k8s/src/lib.rs` at a time — it is the\ncollision point for s0, s3-feed, s3-drain and s2b (`build_pod`).\n\n\n- **Landed half (in s1's scope):** the manual **pin** (\"keep this Run's\n  workspaces\"), **graceful degradation** (expired inputs widen a rerun's scope\n  and say so, per ADR-0027), a `scarab_workspace_warm_used_bytes` gauge on\n  `/metrics`, and a loud ENOSPC path that logs `warm tier full — serving from\n  cold` rather than failing a Step.\n- **Deferred half:** space-bounded LRU eviction — ticket `24476bc`. The blocker\n  is not the loop: `ObjectStore::list_objects` returns `modified_ms`, i.e.\n  least-recently-**written**, which is the wrong key for immutable\n  content-addressed blobs. Real LRU needs an **access index**, which nothing\n  has.\n\nDo not ship a `WarmTier` impl you cannot exercise.\n\n\nPart 2 (**lazy materialisation**) is the load-bearing part, not part 3. s0's\nfinding 2 — the CAS cost tracks **file count**, not bytes (an 8× file-count\nreduction at identical bytes cut `cas_ingest` 4.4× and `cas_materialize` 5.0×)\n— means the win is in not making the round trip at all. Deleting the tunnel is\nstill right (unresumable, abuses the API server, blocks lazy reads) but on its\nown buys roughly a tenth of the boundary. Corollary for s1: **whatever the\nworkspace service does, it must not reproduce a per-file sequential round-trip\nwalk.**\n\n\n| id | title |\n|---|---|\n| `0628369` | ADR-0061 s2b: CSI ephemeral inline volume + FUSE lazy lower layer |\n| `7f05f39` | ADR-0061 s3-drain: replace the drain tunnel and the artifact-harvest tunnel |\n| `24476bc` | ADR-0061: warm-tier space-bounded LRU eviction |\n| `52ef3aa` | ADR-0061: tighten workspace-service blob reads from authenticated to authorized |\n\nEach carries the literal spec — YAML, trait signatures, mount options — so the\nimplementing agent does not re-derive it. `0628369` in particular carries the\n`fsGroupPolicy: None` **ownership contract**, which is the single\nhighest-probability silent failure in the whole design: `fsGroupPolicy: File`\nwould make the kubelet recursively chown the mount and thereby force full\nmaterialisation, destroying laziness — so the driver must itself create\n`upperdir` as `65532:65532` mode `02775` and have the FUSE layer report\n`uid=65532 gid=65532` and `mode | 0o070`, synthesising what `chmod -R g+rwX`\ndoes over `exec` today (git-bug b04697f).\n\n\n| id | severity | title |\n|---|---|---|\n| `a3e7845` | **HIGH** | a truncated workspace drain tar publishes a PARTIAL tree as the step's authoritative snapshot |\n| `4ab5286` | low | two comments claim shipped features are unimplemented; one is an ignored test stub that should now be written |\n| `64897db` | low | the workspace feed is implemented twice with a copy-pasted shell command; the debug pod diverges silently |\n| `1344d1d` | cosmetic | Browse reports a symlink as a file and streams its target path as the file's contents |\n\n`a3e7845` is the one to read: `exec_capture_stdout` sets `.stderr(false)` and\nnever calls `take_status()`, and kube-4's `AttachedProcess::join()` returns\n`Err` only for websocket/task failures — the remote command's exit status goes\nto a separate channel. A `tar` that dies at a record boundary unpacks cleanly\ninto a *partial* tree that is then published as\n`scarab.io/workspace-root`. s3-drain deletes that code path, so if s3-drain\nlands live-verified it is moot — until then it is a live silent-data-loss path.\n\n\nIts \"s4 and s8 are not written down anywhere\" hazard is **wrong** — `6b39fa2`\nand `4f15a66` both exist. The audit did not have access to the ticket set.\n\n\n**@thulasi-ram** — 2026-07-27T04:55:02Z\n\n\nThis PR is no longer documentation-only. Slices s7, s0, s1, s3-feed, s4, s5(half) and s8\nlanded, plus one unplanned slice that measurement forced. The CSI/FUSE node driver is\n**deferred with a full spec**, deliberately.\n\n\n**GitHub Actions has been failing account-wide since `2026-07-26T08:39Z`, which predates\nthis work.** Last successful run: `2026-07-26T07:27Z`. Since then every workflow on every\nbranch — including `main` — fails in 2–3 seconds with **`steps: 0`**, i.e. the jobs never\nget a runner. That is an Actions quota / billing block on a private free-plan repo, not a\ncode failure. `just pr-gate 93` therefore reports all five required checks red and **cannot\ngo green until the Actions quota is restored.**\n\nLocal verification is the only signal available, so here it is in full:\n\n| gate | local result |\n|---|---|\n| `ci/test` (`just test`, real Postgres) | **771 passed**, 0 failed, 25 skipped (`#[ignore]`d live tiers) |\n| `ci/openapi-drift` | **no drift** — regenerated `openapi.json` and the TS client, `git status` clean |\n| `ci/ui-tests` (`just ui-test`) | **100 passed** / 7 files |\n| `ci/coverage` ratchet | **PASS** vs the committed baseline; every touched crate up (`storage-s3` +15.5pp, `server` +5.5pp, `executor-k8s` +3.3pp), worst delta −0.3pp on an untouched crate, inside the 0.5pp slack |\n| `kind/cluster-tests` | **19 run, 17 passed, 0 skipped** on real kind/colima. The 2 failures are contention flakes on a box also hosting the Helm dogfood stack; both pass in isolation and neither builds a workspace CAS, so `poll` never enters `drive_workspace` |\n| `cargo check --workspace` | clean, **0 warnings** |\n| `tsc --noEmit` | clean |\n\n---\n\n\ns0 instrumented the Step boundary and committed the numbers (`bd30a71`) *before* s3-feed\ndeleted the instrumentation:\n\n| leg | files | exec tunnel | server tar | **CAS ↔ object store** | CAS share |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| feed | 2000 | 1244 ms | 1102 ms | **11581 ms** | **83%** |\n| drain | 2000 | 385 ms | 939 ms | **8867 ms** | **87%** |\n\n**The `kubectl exec` tar tunnel this ADR was written to delete is 4–15% of a Step\nboundary.** The CAS legs were 81–88%, because `materialize` awaited `get_blob` **per file,\nserially**, and `ingest_dir` awaited `head`+`put` **per file, serially** — one HTTP\nround-trip per file per direction, against *loopback*. Measured dedup saved **zero**\nwall-clock, because `put_if_absent` pays a `head` per file either way.\n\nThis does not reopen the decision — the rejection is structural, and s0's own ticket says\nits purpose is to *\"size the win and sequence the slices\"*. So it resequenced them. An\nunplanned slice fixed the actual bottleneck:\n\n| leg | before | after (concurrency 32) | speed-up |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| cold `ingest` | 12277 ms | **4540 ms** | 2.7× |\n| `materialize` | 10145 ms | **1096 ms** | **9.3×** |\n\nMeasured as a controlled A/B against the *actual pre-change commit* in a detached worktree,\nnot by setting the knob to 1 — though `c=1` matching the old code to 1% is what establishes\nit was one variable. Honest caveat recorded in the ADR: `tar_pack` **rose** 1102 → ~4700 ms\non code nobody touched, because a slow `materialize` used to absorb the page-cache\nwriteback and a fast one doesn't. So the feed *boundary* improved 2.0×, not 6.2×.\n\nTwo consequences now in the ADR: **part 2 (lazy materialisation) is the load-bearing part,\nnot part 3**; and the parked node-blob-cache question is answered **no, not yet** — it is a\nsecond-order fix that would be credited with wins belonging to the cheaper one.\n\n---\n\n\n- **A directory symlink failed the entire drain.** `ingest_dir` used `DirEntry::file_type`\n  (an `lstat`), so `lib64 -\u003e lib` was not `is_dir()`, fell through to `fs::read`, hit\n  `EISDIR`, and retried forever until the poison ceiling. Any venv or `.bin` layout. Fixed\n  in `dd67e12`.\n- **The exec bit was lost at every Step boundary.** `TreeEntry` carried no mode and no\n  mtime; `materialize` used `fs::write` → `0644`. The feed's `chmod -R g+rwX` cannot restore\n  what was never stored. This was ADR-0061's flagged-unverified item and the answer was \"no\".\n- **A truncated drain tar published a partial tree as a step's authoritative snapshot, with\n  a green verdict.** `exec_capture_stdout` set `.stderr(false)` and never called\n  `take_status()`; kube-4's `join()` returns `Err` only for websocket failures. Fixed with\n  both a status check *and* a stream sentinel — an absent status frame is legitimately torn\n  down with the container, so refusing on absence would only buy false reds. git-bug\n  `a3e7845`, closed.\n- **`just demo` had been broken since ADR-0039 landed** — `busybox:latest` runs as root and\n  the `runAsNonRoot` baseline refused the container.\n- **A `416` for non-empty blobs.** `metadata().unwrap_or(0)` after a successful `File::open`\n  made every range unsatisfiable and returned a terminal \"this object is empty\". A 500 gets\n  retried; a 416 does not.\n- **The live tier could report PASS while executing nothing.** `cluster.rs` fixtures used\n  `else { return }` — exit 0 — on a missing env var, and **nothing set the three new\n  workspace vars**, so seven live tests no-opped, five of which had been green on `main`.\n  `kind/cluster-tests` is `required-if-run`, so the gate saw green. The same shape existed on\n  five other vars. All now panic when the tier is opted in, so deleting an env line from\n  `kind.yml` turns the tier red.\n\n---\n\n\ns7 put `mtime_ms` in the canonical tree JSON — which **is** the hash preimage. It defended\nthis by arguing skip-if-unchanged only compares roots of upstream steps that *did not*\nre-run. That is wrong: the entire point of skip-if-unchanged is the case where an ancestor\n**did** re-run and produced identical output. The live tier proved it — two Attempts,\nbyte-identical content, roots differing in exactly `mtime_ms`.\n\nFixed as **two digests, one address**: the snapshot root stays the only address; a *content\nidentity* (the same merkle fold with mtimes dropped) is what invalidation compares. Simply\ndropping mtime from the hash was rejected — under `put_if_absent` the first-stored wins, so\na path whose content went X→Y→X is served a **stale mtime from another lineage**, and an\nmtime-based build tool skips a rebuild it needed. Preimage bytes are unchanged, so **nothing\nstored is orphaned**.\n\nWhile confirming this we found the chronology runs the other way from doc drift:\n**ADR-0027's amendment was wrong on the day it was written, by twelve days** —\nskip-if-unchanged shipped `7ea905d` on 07-12, the amendment declared it unbuilt on 07-24,\nand s7 on 07-27 retroactively made it true in effect. `rerun_step` genuinely compares\nnothing; the comparison is in *admission*, ~1000 lines away.\n\n---\n\n\nThree ADR statements were false and are corrected in this PR (`fd2b6ad`):\n\n- ~~\"Browse reads snapshots from the service\"~~ — it read the object store directly. **Now\n  true**: the control plane's store is `TieredCas{warm: WorkspaceClient, cold: object\n  store}` with cold fall-through, and `browse_claims` is minted per request.\n- ~~\"The `kubectl exec` tar tunnel is deleted\"~~ — the **feed** tunnel is. The **drain**\n  tunnel and a **third** `exec` tar in `harvest_artifacts` (which the ADR never mentioned)\n  remain, deferred as s3-drain.\n- ~~\"The Helm-dogfood failure class disappears… a real volume removes that entirely\"~~ —\n  misattributed. The durable copy lives in **cold**; what fixes `local-helm` is `deploy.sh`\n  deploying MinIO. The warm PV \"promises nothing\" by this ADR's own retention table, so it\n  cannot be what removes a data-loss class.\n\n`CONTEXT.md` §5's diagram was the worst offender and is fixed: the only edge into the\nservice was labelled \"root hashes only (tens of bytes)\", when the Pod↔service edge is the\none carrying bulk data — and no bulk edge from the executor to object storage was drawn at\nall, so the diagram asserted part 3 was complete.\n\n**Nothing is lazy yet.** Laziness requires the privileged mount, i.e. s2b. `s3-feed` is the\nADR's own sanctioned stepping stone, ordered **in time** rather than as a runtime fallback —\na fallback is two mental models and would calcify — with three anti-calcification guards: an\n`eager` log line and counter on every Pod, a `DELETE ME with the driver (git-bug 0628369)`\nmarker at all eight sites, and a **hard gate** in `up.sh`.\n\nAlso unverified: anything in `deploy/local-helm` (chart renders and `helm lint` passes, but\nno StatefulSet was created and **no PVC has ever bound**); the CI wiring for the live tier\n(YAML-valid and mirrors `up.sh`'s proven shape, but the \"host process reachable at the kind\ngateway\" assumption is a Linux-runner property only testable through the darwin equivalent);\nand whether `/flat` + `have` + parallel GETs beats the old tunnel in wall-clock — s0 gave\nthe before-number, there is no after-number, and s0 says the tunnel was only 4–15% anyway.\n\n---\n\n\n`0628369` s2b (CSI ephemeral inline volume + FUSE lazy lower layer — carries the full YAML,\nthe two-crate split with `exclude` and why `cfg`-gating was rejected, the overlay mount\noptions with per-option justification, and the **`fsGroupPolicy: None` ownership contract**,\nflagged as the single highest-probability silent failure: `fsGroupPolicy: File` would make\nthe kubelet recursively chown the mount and thereby force full materialisation, destroying\nlaziness) · `7f05f39` s3-drain · `66fc0e3` s6 overlay-diff drain · `24476bc` warm-tier LRU\n(blocked on wanting an **access index** — `list_objects` gives last-*written*, the wrong key\nfor immutable content) · `52ef3aa` tighten blob reads from authenticated to authorized ·\n`9b795be` live-verify identity determinism · `4f15a66` the remaining naming debt.\n\nOne ticket was filed and then **closed as not-a-bug**: `0e8f1f3` claimed `image.yml` would\nnot rebuild the fetcher. The `paths:` filter sits under `pull_request:` only and already\nincludes `crates/**`, while `push: branches: [main]` has no filter at all. Second time in\nthis repo a blocker got recorded without checking the trigger primitives.","files":null}]}